<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ashoka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 232 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ashoka was a Maurya emperor who ruled most of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-subcontinent">Indian subcontinent</a> in the third century BCE and died in 232 BCE. After his bloody conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalinga">Kalinga</a> he embraced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> and promoted a policy of dhamma, publicised through inscriptions on rocks and pillars. His reign marked the peak of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>, which declined soon after his death.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ashoka.jpg" alt="Ashoka" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo Dharma from Sadao, Thailand · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ashoka%27s_visit_to_the_Ramagrama_stupa_Sanchi_Stupa_1_Southern_gateway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
